{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1795268198,"nonce":"gHKryNa9OdmlhwfAUDotSEnm4H0=","message":"Marking and allocation work. Measured on the x86 NUC (i5-1250P) at 97% idle, 5 interleaved runs, medians.\n\nThree causes found, all fixed.\n\n1. A word outside the heap went through a binary search over the large-block table. A heap with any large block paid this for every integer, float and static address it scanned. The pool rejects such a word with one range compare. The marker now keeps the lowest and highest large-block address and tests them in the scan loop. The scan loop also tests the heap range inline, instead of calling `consider` for every word. On tok2 the mark time over the run halved (518 to 235 ms). A synthetic graph with 64 large blocks showed the same cost (8-word objects, 70 to 116 ms) and it is gone.\n\n2. On tok2 about 60% of the in-heap words are interior pointers. Each one walked back through up to 8 start bytes. One unaligned 8-byte load and a leading-zero count now find the nearest start. A unit test checks it against the byte walk over a random start table.\n\n3. The small-object fast path lived in one function with the large and medium paths. Every call paid 6 pushes, a 168-byte frame and a stack copy of the region. `alloc` now bumps the thread-local region directly and calls `alloc_general`, which is not inlined, for everything else.\n\nTried and dropped: a prefetch queue of 8 in front of the scan. It gave no gain on tok2.\n\nWall time in seconds, median (HEAD d2904633, now, pool):\n\n| row | HEAD | now | pool |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| w_phase | 2.10 | 1.64 | 1.43 |\n| w_hold | 2.29 | 1.79 | 1.69 |\n| w_grow | 0.409 | 0.348 | 0.334 |\n| tok2 | 2.47 | 2.31 | 1.96 |\n| binary_trees | 0.182 | 0.171 | 0.148 |\n| tokenize | 0.109 | 0.105 | 0.096 |\n| pipeline | 0.266 | 0.250 | 0.257 |\n| records | 0.096 | 0.090 | 0.084 |\n\nPeak RSS is unchanged on w_hold and binary_trees. On tok2 it is 176 MB now, 213 MB at HEAD and 231 MB for the pool.\n\nStill open. tok2 is the widest row, at 1.18x the pool. Its mark rate is 0.235 ms/MB against 0.194 for the pool. Each collection reads about 8M words and 1.5M of them land in the heap. The per-word cost of the scan loop and the one-byte-per-quantum start table are the next suspects. On w_phase the remaining gap is about 1 ns per allocation. It comes from the indirect call in `zyntax_alloc`, the start-byte store and the 16-byte tail clear.\n\nThe ignored test `crates/compiler/tests/mark_speed.rs` times a collection over a linked graph on either heap. Run it with `ZYNTAX_HOST_HEAP=immix cargo test --release -p zyntax_compiler --test mark_speed -- --ignored --nocapture`.\n\nLanded in 79157817. Lua conformance under Immix passes 46 of 46 on both the Mac and the NUC.","files":null}]}